WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.840
An enzyme beside the kidney's receptor decides which steroid reaches it.

2
00:00:06.380 --> 00:00:10.240
Inside the adrenal gland, enzymes decide which steroids get made.

3
00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:13.820
Some newborns lack one of those enzymes.

4
00:00:15.140 --> 00:00:16.720
Yet one kind of hormone rises.

5
00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:20.400
How can losing an enzyme make more hormone?

6
00:00:23.883 --> 00:00:27.203
In the adrenal cortex, steroids grow like a tree.

7
00:00:28.623 --> 00:00:33.523
Cholesterol enters one shared trunk, and ACTH speeds that slow first step.

8
00:00:34.743 --> 00:00:39.983
Three branches leave it, ending in aldosterone, cortisol and mostly weak androgens.

9
00:00:41.383 --> 00:00:46.903
One enzyme, 21-hydroxylase, sits on the routes to cortisol and aldosterone.

10
00:00:48.223 --> 00:00:50.303
The androgen branch does not need it.

11
00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:54.380
Read any adrenal state in three steps, in this order.

12
00:00:55.740 --> 00:01:01.120
One, the enzymes: a missing enzyme lowers everything after it, and backs up what

13
00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:02.120
comes just before.

14
00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:06.660
Two, the receptors: what can the steroids now present do?

15
00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:09.180
Three, only then, the loops.

16
00:01:10.283 --> 00:01:16.463
Take classic salt-wasting 21-hydroxylase deficiency, where enzyme activity is near

17
00:01:16.463 --> 00:01:22.063
zero. Step one. Cortisol and aldosterone fall.

18
00:01:23.443 --> 00:01:28.163
17-hydroxyprogesterone, the substrate just before the block, piles up.

19
00:01:29.343 --> 00:01:30.543
It is the screening marker.

20
00:01:31.667 --> 00:01:38.347
Step two. Nothing that piles up can replace aldosterone, because the block comes

21
00:01:38.347 --> 00:01:40.967
before the precursor that acts like aldosterone.

22
00:01:42.527 --> 00:01:48.387
Worse, backed-up progesterone and 17-hydroxyprogesterone weakly block the

23
00:01:48.387 --> 00:01:49.847
mineralocorticoid receptor.

24
00:01:51.027 --> 00:01:52.547
They can add to the salt loss.

25
00:01:53.617 --> 00:01:55.017
Step three, the loops.

26
00:01:56.257 --> 00:02:01.457
Low cortisol releases the brake on ACTH, which drives the shared trunk harder.

27
00:02:02.737 --> 00:02:06.757
The extra flow spills into the one branch still open: the androgens.

28
00:02:08.117 --> 00:02:11.497
They rise, and long ACTH drive enlarges the gland.

29
00:02:13.257 --> 00:02:16.257
Meanwhile sodium is lost, so volume and pressure fall.

30
00:02:17.300 --> 00:02:21.280
Now use the rule on two adults who both make too much cortisol.

31
00:02:22.820 --> 00:02:25.460
In one, the pituitary makes too much ACTH.

32
00:02:26.860 --> 00:02:30.480
In the other, an adrenal adenoma makes cortisol on its own.

33
00:02:31.820 --> 00:02:34.260
Which way do the adrenal androgens go in each?

34
00:02:40.267 --> 00:02:45.767
With too much ACTH, the whole trunk is driven, so androgens rise with cortisol.

35
00:02:47.207 --> 00:02:50.067
The adenoma's cortisol suppresses ACTH.

36
00:02:51.407 --> 00:02:55.087
The androgen-making zone goes quiet with it, so androgens fall.

37
00:02:56.447 --> 00:03:00.787
Both have high cortisol, yet their androgens move in opposite directions.

38
00:03:02.107 --> 00:03:03.987
The difference is step three, the loops.

39
00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:06.980
So start where the pathway breaks.

40
00:03:08.140 --> 00:03:10.740
Then ask what the steroids left behind can do.

41
00:03:11.860 --> 00:03:13.500
Let the feedback respond last.

42
00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:19.420
This rule predicts each level's direction compared with a healthy person, not its

43
00:03:19.420 --> 00:03:19.740
size.
